h as  oblong Industries director of design and  computation before I joined Academia and  at oblong I worked with an  interdisciplinary team of designers and  programmers to build gestural interfaces  and Conference Room collaboration  products like mezzanine which is  pictured uh in this slide  and after working in the software  industry for a while I started to wonder  about what would happen if I were able  to make some time to focus on personal  work and so I went back to graduate  school at UCLA where I just studied  design media arts and more recently I've  been making work about phones gestures  and surveillance which is what most of  this talk will focus on  so some guiding questions for my  practice are what happens as computers  know and learn more about human bodies  what freedoms and privacies are  jeopardized or lost altogether and how  do human bodies change under the  pressures of emerged virtual and  physical reality and as a disclaimer I  won't really answer these questions in  the talk but they're what I think about  when I'm making work  so when I went to graduate school it  really re-situated my expectations for  how you use mobile devices and how and  when it's appropriate to use a mobile  device so surely I had seen some uh and  used mine in meetings at work and  multitasked like to do things like  checking email during meetings or other  secondary activities but for some reason  I failed to expect that that would also  be true in a university setting and I  couldn't believe that people were just  checking Instagram during class and this  is because I hadn't been in school when  the iPhone became widely adopted so this  change of context for me from going to  Industry and back to school kind of took  mobile devices and made them like  conspicuous to me again and made them  strange to me so I really really  couldn't get them off my mind  and relatedly Wendy Chun writes that  habits are strange contradictory things  they are human-made nature or 