ere are some stills from various times  of day this is around 9 15 in the  morning  and this is around 10 in the morning  not an uncommon time for me to be in the  car I guess  and 11 15  around noon  and around 8 30 at night and generally  in the evenings there were less Clips  because I would be busy with other  people around and not in my phone as  much or as the morning hours tended to  have a lot more activity  and here's uh one around midnight  so here's a clip of the video around 7  45 in the morning  which was apparently a very common time  for me to wake up while I was in  graduate school  and very quietly from my computer I can  hear the unlock sound playing  [Laughter]  laughs  cat is very cuddly  and as soon as she hears the alarm goes  off she wants me to know she's available  so looking at both of these works and  reflecting on them I felt that they both  contained a sort of inherent judgment  that maybe the phone is pulling us out  of physical space and overwhelming our  senses  and the reality is our reality is more  complex than that our lives are both  simultaneously online and offline so the  videos I felt were a little misleading  because they make it look like nothing's  happening but really all the action at  these times is on the device itself  so from here I worked on a new version  of the software that would also capture  the screen to see if that would present  a more complete picture of the  activities that were taking place and  while working on previous versions of  the project I learned a lot about video  encoding and uh was able to capture  higher resolution video to work in this  iterate to use in this next iteration of  the project and this time I also had a  visual style in mind for combining all  three of the video feeds  so here's a different idea of what  waking up in the morning is like  and there's a lot of sensitive  information here it feels really  intimate to see the names of people in  my inbox and Instagram feed while I'm