metimes  they would be really different  so here are some Stills looking a little  bit different as before there are some  like awkward moments like eating or  weird angles  and some sequences are resting at home  like this one on the left where I am  doing the New York Times crossword  puzzle uh as I lay on my couch the one  in the middle I'm out grocery shopping  and have my grocery list up and in the  third one I'm doing uh some shopping for  some intimate personal items while I  text one of my friends to see what  they're up to later  other parts of this project might reveal  personal details like here are some  articles that I was reading here's one  that's a little meta where my friend is  uh posting on Instagram about using the  software  uh and another one where my roommate at  the time is asking me about a recent  date  so here are some of them in motion and  in these uh three Clips uh all three of  us are  um  using another device in the background  so like there's another computer being  depicted  and accounting for data I saw lots of  different examples of Biometrics being  used some things that were measurable  some things like one of my absolute  favorite Clips is the one in the middle  here where my friend is brushing her  teeth and she kind of disappears for a  while and then here's like the phone  make a sound and comes back to see  what's going on and in the third one  here Google Maps is up while my friend  drives to another location  so here's what the insulation looked  like with all three videos together  and something that struck me working on  this project was that it wasn't just the  voluntary participants in this work who  were implicated it was also everyone  that we knew from a distance and this is  the nature of our existence online uh so  we it's not possible to opt out of every  single transaction or interaction that  we might have with someone you however  large or small it may be  and the process for filming this piece  was a little 